INFO 03-13 07:53:56 logger.py:39] Received request cmpl-a170f301bb5a4e71b45fb7b400a7d21a-0: prompt: 'Serializability is a property of schedules in a database management system that ensures that the schedule is equivalent to some serial schedule. In other words, it ensures that the schedule does not produce any conflicts and it respects the ACID properties.\n\n1. Conflict Serializability: A schedule is said to be conflict-serializable if it can be transformed into a serial schedule by reordering the operations, without violating the ACID properties. A schedule is conflict-serializable if it is equivalent to a schedule that is obtained by executing the operations of each transaction in some order, such that no two operations in the schedule conflict with each other.\n2. View Serializability: A schedule is said to be view serializable if it is equivalent to a schedule that is obtained by executing the operations of each transaction in some order, such that the result of the schedule is the same as if the operations were executed in some serial order, even if the operations are not conflict-serializable.\n3. Cascaded Aborts: It refers to the situation when the execution of a transaction is unsuccessful and it causes other transactions to also be rolled back or aborted. This can happen because the failed transaction may have modified data that other transactions rely on.\n4. Recoverable Schedule: A schedule is said to be recoverable if the database remains in a consistent state after the execution of the schedule, even if some transactions are rolled back or aborted.\n5. Non-recoverable Schedule: A schedule is said to be non-recoverable if the database is left in an inconsistent state after the execution of the schedule, due to some transactions being rolled back or aborted.\n\nIn short, Serializability is a property that ensures that a schedule does not produce any conflicts and respects the ACID properties. Conflict serializability and view serializability are different ways to ensure the serializability of a schedule. Cascaded aborts refer to the situation where the failure of one transaction causes other transactions to also be rolled back or aborted. Recoverable schedule is the one that leaves the database in a consistent state after execution, while non-recoverable schedule leaves the database in an inconsistent state.', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=-1, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=True, max_tokens=256, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None), prompt_token_ids: [5915, 449, 2897, 374, 264, 3343, 315, 36769, 304, 264, 4625, 6240, 1849, 429, 25351, 429, 279, 9700, 374, 13578, 311, 1045, 6146, 9700, 13, 758, 1008, 4244, 11, 432, 25351, 429, 279, 9700, 1558, 537, 8193, 894, 25800, 323, 432, 42566, 279, 10584, 915, 5888, 382, 16, 13, 58585, 11215, 449, 2897, 25, 362, 9700, 374, 1053, 311, 387, 12055, 12, 10182, 8335, 421, 432, 646, 387, 23507, 1119, 264, 6146, 9700, 553, 312, 51869, 279, 7525, 11, 2041, 44362, 279, 10584, 915, 5888, 13, 362, 9700, 374, 12055, 12, 10182, 8335, 421, 432, 374, 13578, 311, 264, 9700, 429, 374, 12180, 553, 30220, 279, 7525, 315, 1817, 7745, 304, 1045, 1973, 11, 1741, 429, 902, 1378, 7525, 304, 279, 9700, 12055, 448, 1817, 1008, 624, 17, 13, 2738, 11215, 449, 2897, 25, 362, 9700, 374, 1053, 311, 387, 1651, 6146, 8335, 421, 432, 374, 13578, 311, 264, 9700, 429, 374, 12180, 553, 30220, 279, 7525, 315, 1817, 7745, 304, 1045, 1973, 11, 1741, 429, 279, 1102, 315, 279, 9700, 374, 279, 1852, 438, 421, 279, 7525, 1033, 15695, 304, 1045, 6146, 1973, 11, 1496, 421, 279, 7525, 525, 537, 12055, 12, 10182, 8335, 624, 18, 13, 95006, 14255, 3680, 18955, 25, 1084, 19257, 311, 279, 6534, 979, 279, 11320, 315, 264, 7745, 