on women artists marks a significant shift in the museum's project. The forthcoming rehang of its collection will feature 800 works by more than 350 artists, including 50% female artists. This rehang is the first in a decade and represents a new chapter for the museum.\n\nAlex Farquharson, the director of the Tate Britain, says that the new rehang will enable visitors to delve deeper into the narrative of British art and consider how it speaks to us, challenges us, and inspires us. The Tate Britain is committed to showcasing a wealth of Britain's art diversity, including art from 500 years ago.\n\nThe museum will highlight both historically significant artworks and artists, such as John Constable and Henry Moore, as well as new and contemporary artists. For example, Rachel Jones's colorful acrylics and Rene Matić's photography will have their place in the Tate Britain. Andrea Schlieker, the director of exhibitions and displays at the Tate Britain, believes that this exhibition can help us reflect on the past and envision a clearer future.\n\nJoan Carlile, the first female professional painter, will also have her work displayed at the museum, as well as many other female artists from different eras and centuries. The Tate Britain's focus on women artists aims to provide a platform for those who have never had the opportunity to display their works before.\n\nThe Tate Britain is not only focusing on the rehang of its collection, but it is also launching another project in May to discuss the relationship between national collection artworks and the country's legacy of colonization and the slave trade. This undertaking is part of the museum's commitment to correctly representing contemporary society norms through its collection.\n\nIn conclusion, the Tate Britain museum's rehang of its collection represents a significant shift in the museum's project, which aims to provide visitors with a more", params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, re