INFO 03-13 07:54:03 logger.py:39] Received request cmpl-e4080b198e1c43a4a057ec8784bdff3e-0: prompt: 'Great! Using serializers is a good way to validate the incoming request data and convert it to the format that your API needs. Here\'s how you can modify the forecast and historical weather APIs to use serializers:\n\n1. In your `forecast` app directory, create a new file called `serializers.py`:\n```python\nfrom rest_framework import serializers\n\nclass ForecastSerializer(serializers.Serializer):\n    city = serializers.CharField()\n\nclass HistoricalWeatherSerializer(serializers.Serializer):\n    city = serializers.CharField()\n    date = serializers.DateField(input_formats=[\'%Y-%m-%d\'])\n```\nThis creates two new serializers called `ForecastSerializer` and `HistoricalWeatherSerializer` that validate the incoming request data and convert it to the format that your API needs.\n\n2. In `views.py`, modify the `ForecastView` and `HistoricalWeatherView` classes to use the new serializers:\n```python\nclass ForecastView(APIView):\n    def post(self, request):\n        serializer = ForecastSerializer(data=request.data)\n        if serializer.is_valid():\n            city = serializer.validated_data[\'city\']\n            data = get_forecast(city)\n            return Response(data)\n        else:\n            return Response(serializer.errors, status=status.HTTP_400_BAD_REQUEST)\n\nclass HistoricalWeatherView(APIView):\n    def post(self, request):\n        serializer = HistoricalWeatherSerializer(data=request.data)\n        if serializer.is_valid():\n            city = serializer.validated_data[\'city\']\n            date = serializer.validated_data[\'date\']\n            data = get_historical_weather(date, city)\n            return Response(data)\n        else:\n            return Response(serializer.errors, status=status.HTTP_400_BAD_REQUEST)\n```\nIn these updated views, the incoming request data is validated using the `ForecastSerializer` and `HistoricalWeatherSerializer`, and the validated data is then used to retrieve the forecast or historical weather data.\n\n3. Test the APIs using a tool like `curl` or a REST client such as Postman. For example:\n```sh\ncurl -X POST -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d \'{"city": "San Francisco"}\' http://localhost:8000/forecast/\n```\nThis sends a POST request to the `/forecast/` URL with a JSON payload containing the name of the city for which to retrieve the forecast.\n```sh\ncurl -X POST -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d \'{"city": "San Francisco", "date": "2022-02-01"}\' http://localhost:8000/history/\n```\nThis sends a POST request to the `/history/` URL with a JSON payload containing both the name of the city and the date for which to retrieve historical weather data.\n\nUsing serializers is a good way to ensure that your API receives the correct data in the correct format, and to make it more robust and easier to maintain.', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=-1, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=True, max_tokens=256, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None), prompt_token_ids: [21396, 0, 12091, 38221, 374, 264, 1661, 1616, 311, 9593, 279, 19393, 1681, 821, 323, 5508, 432, 311, 279, 3561, 429, 697, 5333, 3880, 13, 5692, 594, 1246, 498, 646, 5602, 279, 17595, 323, 13656, 9104, 33356, 311, 990, 38221, 1447, 16, 13, 758, 697, 1565, 58984, 63, 906, 6220, 11, 1855, 264, 501, 1034, 2598, 1565, 10182, 12230, 7197, 63, 510, 73594, 12669, 198, 1499, 2732, 27565, 1159, 38221, 271, 1040, 55675, 13909, 57968, 82209, 982, 262, 3283, 284, 38221, 12639, 2822, 1040, 40043, 28981, 13909, 57968, 82209, 982, 262, 3283, 284, 38221, 12639, 741, 262, 2400, 284, 38221, 74979, 5384, 68351, 13987, 4, 56, 11069