INFO 03-13 07:54:05 logger.py:39] Received request cmpl-68d8d01560484420baeef006304092c9-0: prompt: 'Image fusion, mosaic, and merge are all methods of combining multiple images into a single composite image. However, they differ in the way they combine the images and the purpose for which they are used.\n\n1. Image Fusion: Image fusion is a process that combines multiple images of the same scene, taken at different times or from different sensors, to create a new image that contains more information than the individual images. The process involves aligning the images, selecting the best pixels from each image, and combining them into a single high-resolution image. Image fusion is commonly used in remote sensing applications, such as land-use classification, change detection, and object recognition. In the context of water quality monitoring, image fusion can be used to combine different spectral bands from multiple missions to create a more comprehensive image that provides more information about water quality parameters.\n2. Mosaic: A mosaic is a composite image created by stitching together multiple images of adjacent or overlapping areas to create a larger, seamless image. Mosaics are commonly used in geographic information systems (GIS) applications, such as mapping and land-use planning. In the context of water quality monitoring, mosaic can be used to create a larger image that covers a larger water body, allowing for a more comprehensive view of the water quality.\n3. Merge: Image merging is a simple process of combining two or more images of the same scene into a single image. This process is commonly used in image processing applications, such as image enhancement and filtering. In the context of water quality monitoring, merge can be used to combine multiple images of the same water body taken at different times, to create a time-series image that shows changes in water quality over time.\n\nIn summary, image fusion is used to combine different spectral bands from multiple missions to create a comprehensive image that provides more information about water quality parameters, while mosaics and merges are used to create larger images and time-series images, respectively. The choice of method depends on the specific application and the data available.', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=-1, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=True, max_tokens=256, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None), prompt_token_ids: [1906, 36508, 11, 70524, 11, 323, 10880, 525, 678, 5413, 315, 34171, 5248, 5335, 1119, 264, 3175, 27714, 2168, 13, 4354, 11, 807, 1745, 304, 279, 1616, 807, 15963, 279, 5335, 323, 279, 7428, 369, 892, 807, 525, 1483, 382, 16, 13, 4654, 42237, 25, 4654, 36508, 374, 264, 1882, 429, 32411, 5248, 5335, 315, 279, 1852, 6109, 11, 4429, 518, 2155, 3039, 476, 504, 2155, 25092, 11, 311, 1855, 264, 501, 2168, 429, 5610, 803, 1995, 1091, 279, 3842, 5335, 13, 576, 1882, 17601, 5285, 287, 279, 5335, 11, 26301, 279, 1850, 15751, 504, 1817, 2168, 11, 323, 34171, 1105, 1119, 264, 3175, 1550, 62936, 2168, 13, 4654, 36508, 374, 16626, 1483, 304, 8699, 59099, 8357, 11, 1741, 438, 4268, 24673, 23850, 11, 2297, 17984, 11, 323, 1633, 17843, 13, 758, 279, 2266, 315, 3015, 4271, 16558, 11, 2168, 36508, 646, 387, 1483, 311, 15963, 2155, 55977, 20892, 504, 5248, 24640, 311, 1855, 264, 803, 15817, 2168, 429, 5707, 803, 1995, 911, 3015, 4271, 5029, 624, 17, 13, 386, 44783, 25, 362, 70524, 374, 264, 27714, 2168, 3465, 553, 79131, 3786, 5248, 5335, 315, 23942, 476, 49817, 5671, 311, 1855, 264, 8131, 11, 46870, 2168, 13, 386, 11983, 1211, 525, 16626, 1483, 304, 45039, 1995, 5942, 320, 55289, 8, 8357, 11, 1741, 438, 12731, 323, 4268, 24673, 9115, 13, 758, 279,