INFO 03-13 07:54:15 logger.py:39] Received request cmpl-fb55cf59c4f3442b884bbcbc5bd11352-0: prompt: "The formula w\\_i = w\\_i \\* exp(-alpha \\* y\\_i \\* h(x\\_i)) / Z is used to adjust the weights of the training examples in AdaBoost (Adaptive Boosting), a popular boosting algorithm in machine learning.\n\nHere's a brief explanation of each term in the formula:\n\n* w\\_i: The weight of training example i.\n* alpha: A parameter that controls the contribution of each model to the final prediction. Higher values of alpha give more weight to the current model, while lower values give more weight to previous models.\n* y\\_i: The true label of training example i.\n* h(x\\_i): The prediction of the previous model on training example i.\n* Z: A normalization factor that ensures the weights sum to one.\n\nThe term exp(-alpha \\* y\\_i \\* h(x\\_i)) can be thought of as a measure of how much the current model contributes to the error of training example i. If the current model predicts the correct label for example i (i.e., y\\_i \\* h(x\\_i) > 0), then this term will be close to zero, and the weight of example i will remain the same. However, if the current model predicts the wrong label for example i (i.e., y\\_i \\* h(x\\_i) < 0), then this term will be greater than one, and the weight of example i will be increased.\n\nThe normalization factor Z is used to ensure that the weights sum to one, which is necessary for the weights to be interpretable as a probability distribution. The formula for Z depends on the sum of the weights and the values of alpha, y\\_i, and h(x\\_i) for each example in the training set.\n\nThe idea behind this weight update formula is to give more weight to the examples that are misclassified by the previous model, so that the next model will focus more on those examples and try to correct the errors of the previous model. This iterative process of adjusting the weights and training new models leads to improved performance over time, and is the key idea behind AdaBoost and many other boosting algorithms.", params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=-1, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=True, max_tokens=256, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None), prompt_token_ids: [785, 14806, 289, 75738, 72, 284, 289, 75738, 72, 1124, 9, 1343, 4080, 7141, 1124, 9, 379, 75738, 72, 1124, 9, 305, 2075, 75738, 72, 593, 608, 1863, 374, 1483, 311, 7500, 279, 14324, 315, 279, 4862, 10295, 304, 51149, 52363, 320, 2589, 27781, 33407, 287, 701, 264, 5411, 54928, 12111, 304, 5662, 6832, 382, 8420, 594, 264, 9814, 16148, 315, 1817, 4647, 304, 279, 14806, 1447, 9, 289, 75738, 72, 25, 576, 4680, 315, 4862, 3110, 600, 624, 9, 8287, 25, 362, 5733, 429, 11574, 279, 18527, 315, 1817, 1614, 311, 279, 1590, 19639, 13, 34221, 2750, 315, 8287, 2968, 803, 4680, 311, 279, 1482, 1614, 11, 1393, 4722, 2750, 2968, 803, 4680, 311, 3681, 4119, 624, 9, 379, 75738, 72, 25, 576, 830, 2383, 315, 4862, 3110, 600, 624, 9, 305, 2075, 75738, 72, 1648, 576, 19639, 315, 279, 3681, 1614, 389, 4862, 3110, 600, 624, 9, 1863, 25, 362, 48723, 8168, 429, 25351, 279, 14324, 2629, 311, 825, 382, 785, 4647, 1343, 4080, 7141, 1124, 9, 379, 75738, 72, 1124, 9, 305, 2075, 75738, 72, 593, 646, 387, 3381, 315, 438, 264, 6629, 315, 1246, 1753, 279, 1482, 1614, 42972, 311, 279, 1465, 315, 4862, 3110, 600, 13, 1416, 279, 1482, 1614, 55878, 279, 4396, 2383, 369, 3110, 600, 320, 72, 1734, 2572, 379, 75738, 72, 1124, 9, 305, 2075, 75738, 72, 8, 861, 220, 15, 701, 1221, 419, 4647, 686, 387, 3265, 311, 7168, 11, 323, 279, 4680, 315, 3110, 600, 686, 7146, 279, 1852, 13, 4354, 11, 421, 279, 1482, 1614, 55878, 279, 4969, 2383, 369, 3110, 600, 320, 72, 1734, 2572, 379, 75738, 72, 1124, 9, 305, 2075, 757