INFO 03-13 07:54:15 logger.py:39] Received request cmpl-0b2b7ae9d16944c0ae9905cdeb5c3873-0: prompt: 'If you have an XGBoost regression model trained in Python and you want to use it in a .NET application, you can convert it to ONNX format and then use the `Microsoft.ML.OnnxRuntime` package in .NET to load and use the model.\n\nHere\'s a high-level overview of the steps involved:\n\n1. Train and save the XGBoost model: First, train and save your XGBoost model in Python using the XGBoost library\'s built-in functions or third-party libraries such as `xgboost4j`. You can use any of the standard methods to save the model, such as using the `pickle` module or the `joblib` library.\n2. Convert the XGBoost model to ONNX format: Once you have saved your XGBoost model, you can convert it to ONNX format using the `sklearn-onnx` package in Python. This package provides tools to convert XGBoost models to ONNX format.\n3. Load the ONNX model in .NET: Once you have the ONNX model file, you can use the `Microsoft.ML.OnnxRuntime` package in .NET to load the model. The `OnnxModel` class provides a convenient way to load the ONNX model.\n4. Use the ONNX model in your .NET application: With the ONNX model loaded in .NET, you can use it in your application just like any other machine learning model.\n\nHere\'s some example code to get you started:\n\nIn Python:\n```python\nimport joblib\nimport xgboost\nfrom skl2onnx import convert_xgboost\nfrom skl2onnx.common.data_types import FloatTensorType\n\n# Load the XGBoost model\nmodel = joblib.load("model.pkl")\n\n# Convert the model to ONNX format\ninitial_type = [(\'input\', FloatTensorType([None, 13]))]\nonnx_model = convert_xgboost(model, "model", initial_types=initial_type)\n\n# Save the ONNX model\nwith open("model.onnx", "wb") as f:\n    f.write(onnx_model.SerializeToString())\n```\nIn .NET:\n```csharp\nusing System;\nusing System.Linq;\nusing Microsoft.ML.OnnxRuntime;\nusing Microsoft.ML.OnnxRuntime.Tensors;\n\nclass Program\n{\n    static void Main(string[] args)\n    {\n        // Load the ONNX model\n        var session = new InferenceSession("model.onnx");\n\n        // Create an input tensor\n        var tensor = new DenseTensor<float>(new[] { 1, 13 });\n        tensor[0, 0] = 6.2f;\n        tensor[0, 1] = 0.0f;\n        tensor[0, 2] = 0.48f;\n        tensor[0, 3] = 1.8f;\n        tensor[0, 4] = 0.075f;\n        tensor[0, 5] = 15.0f;\n        tensor[0, 6] = 54.0f;\n        tensor[0, 7] = 0.9966f;\n        tensor[0, 8] = 3.59f;\n        tensor[0, 9] = 0.68f;\n        tensor[0, 10] = 10.2f;\n        tensor[0, 11] = 0.44f;\n        tensor[0, 12] = 9.4f;\n\n        // Run the model on the input tensor\n        var inputs = new[] { "input" };\n        var outputs = session.Run(inputs, new[] { tensor }).ToList();\n\n        // Print the output\n```', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=-1, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=True, max_tokens=256, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None), prompt_token_ids: [2679, 498, 614, 458, 1599, 38, 52363, 30549, 1614, 16176, 304, 13027, 323, 498, 1366, 311, 990, 432, 304, 264, 659, 15373, 3766, 11, 498, 646, 5508, 432, 311, 6197, 43304, 3561, 323, 1221, 990, 279, 1565, 12778, 1321, 43, 8071, 23357, 15123, 63, 6328, 304, 659, 15373, 311, 2795, 323, 990, 279, 1614, 382, 8420, 594, 264, 1550, 11591, 23251, 315, 279, 7354, 6398, 1447, 16, 13, 26125, 323, 3581, 279, 1599, 38, 52363, 1614, 25, 5512, 11, 5426, 323, 3581, 697, 1599, 38, 52363, 1614, 304, 13027, 1667, 279, 1599, 38, 52363, 6733, 594, 5798, 3419, 5746, 476, 4843, 24031, 20186, 1741, 438, 1565, 87, 70, 13000, 19, 73, 28587, 1446, 646, 990, 894, 315, 279, 5297, 5413, 311, 3581, 279, 1614, 11, 1741, 438, 1667, 279, 15