INFO 03-13 07:54:24 logger.py:39] Received request cmpl-63c997ecd6e84c5091be33e1070d4458-0: prompt: "Sure, here is an explanation of how Facebook translations process works:\n\nLocalization Strategy:\nFacebook's localization strategy is to make its platform available to users in their native language to improve user experience and engagement. The platform supports over 100 languages and has a large user community that contributes to the translation effort.\n\nTeams:\nFacebook has a dedicated Localization team that manages the translation and localization process. The team includes project managers, translators, and reviewers, who work closely with product teams to ensure the accuracy and quality of translations.\n\nTMS:\nFacebook uses its own in-house Translation Management System (TMS) called FBLearner Flow to manage the translation workflow. FBLearner Flow is an AI-powered system that automatically suggests translations based on previous translations and user data.\n\nMT:\nFacebook uses machine translation (MT) technology to speed up the translation process. It uses both rule-based and neural machine translation (NMT) models to translate content into multiple languages. Facebook's MT models are trained on large amounts of user-generated data to improve their accuracy.\n\nTM:\nFacebook has a Translation Memory (TM) system that stores all translated content for reuse in future translations. The TM system ensures consistency in translations and reduces the time and cost of translation projects.\n\nStandards and Guidelines:\nFacebook has established a set of localization standards and guidelines to ensure the quality and consistency of translations. The guidelines cover tone, style, terminology, and other aspects of translation.\n\nI18N Tools:\nFacebook uses a variety of internationalization (I18N) tools to prepare content for translation. These tools ensure that the content is ready for translation and meets the technical requirements for localization.\n\nMethods:\nFacebook uses a combination of human and machine translation methods to translate content. It uses human translators for high-priority content and uses MT for low-priority content. Facebook also employs a crowdsourcing model to engage its user community in the translation effort.\n\nModels:\nFacebook uses both rule-based and neural machine translation (NMT) models to translate content. The rule-based models are used for languages that have complex grammar rules, while NMT models are used for languages with simpler grammar rules.\n\nWorkflows:\nFacebook's translation workflow is managed through its in-house TMS system, FBLearner Flow. The workflow involves several steps, including content preparation, translation, editing, review, and publishing.\n\nTechnics:\nFacebook uses various technologies to optimize the translation process, including AI, machine learning, and natural language processing (NLP). These technologies help to improve the accuracy and efficiency of translations.\n\nVendors:\nFacebook uses a combination of in-house and external vendors for translation projects. The external vendors are carefully vetted to ensure they meet Facebook's quality standards and adhere to its localization guidelines.\n\nReviews/LQA:\nFacebook has a dedicated Quality Assurance (QA) team that reviews translations for accuracy and quality. The QA team uses both automated and manual review processes to ensure that translations meet Facebook's quality standards.\n\nHere is a table summarizing the key components of Facebook's translation process:\n\n| Component | Description |\n| --- | --- |\n| Localization Strategy | Make the platform available to users in their native language |\n| Teams | Dedicated Localization team |\n| TMS | FBLearner Flow |\n| MT | Rule-based and NMT models |\n| TM | Translation Memory system |\n| Standards/Guidelines | Localization guidelines and standards |\n| I18N Tools | Tools to prepare content for translation |\n| Methods | Human and machine translation, crowdsourcing |\n| Models | Rule-based and NMT models |\n| Workflows | Content preparation, translation, editing, review, publishing |\n| Technics | AI, machine learning, NLP |\n| Vendors | In-house and external vendors |", params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=-1, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=True, max_tokens=256, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None), prompt_token_ids: [39814, 11, 1588, 374, 458, 16148, 315, 1246, 5573, 36693, 1882, 4278, 1447, 73614, 27745, 510, 20489, 594, 52304, 8282, 374, 311, 1281, 1181, 5339, 2500, 311, 3847, 304, 862, 9867, 4128, 311, 7269, 1196, 3139, 323, 19805, 13, 576, 5339, 11554, 916, 220, 16, 15, 15, 15459, 323, 702, 264, 3460, 1196, 3942, 429, 42972, 311, 279, 14468, 5041, 382, 60669, 510, 20489, 702, 264, 12235, 80820, 2083, 429, 28872, 279, 14468, 323, 52304, 1882, 13, 576, 2083, 5646, 2390, 19680, 11, 72704, 11, 323, 59038, 11, 879, 975, 15148, 448, 1985, 7263, 311, 5978, 279, 13403, 323, 4271, 315, 36693, 382, 51, 4826, 510, 20489, 5711, 1181, 1828, 304, 35902, 38041, 9551, 739, 320, 51, 4826, 8, 2598, 434, 9389, 682, 1194, 22452, 311, 10091, 279, 14468, 28288, 13, 434, 9389, 682, 1194, 22452, 374, 458, 15235, 40403, 1849, 429, 9463, 13230, 36693, 3118, 389, 3681, 36693, 323, 1196, 821, 382, 8505, 510, 20489, 5711, 5662, 14468, 320, 8505, 8, 5440, 311, 4628, 705, 279, 14468, 1882, 13, 1084, 5711, 2176, 5912, 5980, 323, 29728, 5662, 14468, 320, 45, 8505, 8, 4119, 311, 14683, 2213, 1119, 5248, 15459, 13, 5573, 594, 19087, 4119, 525, 16176, 389, 3460, 14713, 315, 1196, 16185, 821, 311, 7269, 862, 13403, 382, 22034, 510, 20489, 702, 264, 38041, 13850, 320, 22034, 8, 1849, 429, 10533, 678, 24531, 2213, 369, 25978, 304, 3853, 36693, 13, 576