INFO 03-13 07:54:25 logger.py:39] Received request cmpl-df9b3e9c1bbd4d09a3284787afa605f3-0: prompt: 'To implement real-time video streaming from the drone\'s camera to a ground station, we can use a combination of the drone\'s camera, a video encoder, and a wireless communication system such as Wi-Fi or cellular network. Here\'s an example code to demonstrate how this can be achieved using Python and OpenCV:\n```python\nimport cv2\nimport urllib.request\n\n# Open the video stream from the drone\'s camera\nurl = "http://192.168.1.1:8080/?action=stream"  # replace with drone\'s IP address\nstream = urllib.request.urlopen(url)\n\n# Create a VideoCapture object to read from the stream\nvideo_capture = cv2.VideoCapture()\nif not video_capture.open(stream):\n    print("Cannot open video stream")\n\n# Initialize the video encoder\nfourcc = cv2.VideoWriter_fourcc(*\'XVID\')\nout = cv2.VideoWriter(\'output.avi\', fourcc, 20.0, (640, 480))\n\n# Loop to read frames from the stream and encode and send to the ground station\nwhile True:\n    # Read a frame from the stream\n    ret, frame = video_capture.read()\n    if not ret:\n        break\n    \n    # Encode the frame and send it to the ground station\n    out.write(frame)\n    \n    # Show the frame on the drone\'s screen\n    cv2.imshow(\'Drone Camera\', frame)\n    if cv2.waitKey(1) & 0xFF == ord(\'q\'):\n        break\n\n# Release resources\nout.release()\nvideo_capture.release()\ncv2.destroyAllWindows()\n```\nThis code opens the video stream from the drone\'s camera using its IP address and reads frames from the stream using a `VideoCapture` object. The frames are then encoded and sent to the ground station using a `VideoWriter` object. The frames are also displayed on the drone\'s screen using `imshow` function from OpenCV. The code exits when the user presses the \'q\' key.', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=-1, min_p=0.0,