INFO 03-13 07:54:25 logger.py:39] Received request cmpl-67d748dc154d4e85bf9d4b9da0c7a12d-0: prompt: 'Yes, GitVersion should work with squash merges. However, when you perform a squash merge, Git creates a new commit that combines all the changes from the source branch into a single commit, losing the individual commit history of the source branch. As a result, GitVersion might not always be able to determine the correct branch type that was merged, especially when using squash merges with the `tracks-release-branches` option.\n\nTo mitigate this issue, you can include a comment in the commit message during a squash merge that mentions the merged branch. GitVersion uses commit messages to identify the merged branch type when the individual commit history is not available.\n\nHere\'s an example of a squash merge command with a commit message that mentions the merged branch:\n```bash\ngit checkout main\ngit merge --squash feature/my-feature-branch\ngit commit -m "Squash merge of feature/my-feature-branch"\n```\nReplace `feature/my-feature-branch` with the actual name of the feature branch being merged.\n\nBy including the branch name in the commit message, GitVersion should be able to determine the correct branch type and apply the corresponding configuration when calculating the version.\n\nHowever, it is essential to note that using squash merges might still result in some limitations and edge cases when working with GitVersion. If you encounter issues with squash merges and version calculation, consider using regular merge commits or rebase and merge to preserve the individual commit history of merged branches.', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=-1, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=True, max_tokens=256, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_s