INFO 03-13 07:54:26 logger.py:39] Received request cmpl-4efeb1c0f30d4cbb8c4789d6610f9359-0: prompt: 'Certainly, here are some model answers for each of the prompts in the writing activity:\n\n1. How does Priestley use the character of Inspector Goole to challenge the attitudes of the Birling family?\n\nPriestley uses Inspector Goole as a voice of morality and justice in the play. He challenges the Birling family\'s complacency and self-righteousness, exposing their hypocrisy and lack of empathy for the lower classes. The Inspector\'s interrogation techniques and his ability to make the characters confront their own guilt forces them to question their own beliefs and values. Inspector Goole represents Priestley\'s socialist ideals and his belief that individuals should take responsibility for their actions and be accountable to the community as a whole.\n\n1. What is the significance of the setting in Act 1?\n\nThe setting of Act 1, the Birling family\'s dining room, is significant in several ways. Firstly, it represents the comfortable and privileged lifestyle of the Birlings, with its opulent furnishings and expensive decor. Secondly, it creates a sense of claustrophobia and confinement, highlighting the insular and self-absorbed nature of the family. Finally, the setting serves as a backdrop for Priestley\'s exploration of social class and the class divide, as the Birlings are oblivious to the plight of the working class and are more concerned with their own social status.\n\n1. How does Priestley use language and stage directions to create tension in Act 1?\n\nPriestley uses language and stage directions to create a sense of foreboding and tension in Act 1. He often uses short sentences and staccato rhythms to create a sense of urgency and unease. He also employs repetition and rhetorical devices to emphasize certain ideas and themes. For example, the repetition of the phrase "I tell you" by Mr. Birling creates a sense of dogmatism and self-righteousness. The stage directions also contribute to the tension, as characters enter and exit the stage unexpectedly and the lighting changes to reflect the mood of the scene.', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=-1, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=True, max_tokens=256, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None), prompt_token_ids: [95456, 11, 1588, 525, 1045, 1614, 11253, 369, 1817, 315, 279, 50932, 304, 279, 4378, 5702, 1447, 16, 13, 2585, 1558, 48903, 3179, 990, 279, 3668, 315, 44495, 5994, 1263, 311, 8645, 279, 32626, 315, 279, 36819, 2718, 2997, 1939, 92878, 477, 3179, 5711, 44495, 5994, 1263, 438, 264, 7743, 315, 44551, 323, 12161, 304, 279, 1486, 13, 1260, 11513, 279, 36819, 2718, 2997, 594, 4581, 40624, 323, 656, 6701, 33072, 2090, 11, 45966, 862, 71706, 323, 6853, 315, 47351, 369, 279, 4722, 6846, 13, 576, 44495, 594, 65265, 12538, 323, 806, 5726, 311, 1281, 279, 5766, 16877, 862, 1828, 33851, 8437, 1105, 311, 3405, 862, 1828, 20799, 323, 2750, 13, 44495, 5994, 1263, 10868, 48903, 3179, 594, 40189, 51705, 323, 806, 16396, 429, 7775, 1265, 1896, 11752, 369, 862, 6168, 323, 387, 35877, 311, 279, 3942, 438, 264, 4361, 382, 16, 13, 3555, 374, 279, 25361, 315, 279, 6243, 304, 3218, 220, 16, 1939, 785, 6243, 315, 3218, 220, 16, 11, 279, 36819, 2718, 2997, 594, 17914, 3054, 11, 374, 5089, 304, 3807, 5510, 13, 76695, 11, 432, 10868, 279, 10655, 323, 46261, 18899, 315, 279, 425, 2811, 819, 11, 448, 1181, 1179, 28480, 72171, 323, 11392, 10576, 13, 71031, 11, 432, 11450, 264, 5530, 315, 86704, 590, 21990, 40063, 323, 65578, 11, 38586, 279, 1640, 1276, 323, 656, 96410, 269, 2721, 6993, 315, 279, 2997, 13, 17375, 11, 279, 6243, 17045, 438, 264, 38477, 369, 48903, 3179, 594, 26403, 315, 3590, 536, 323,