you can assign permissions to the "Page1 Readers" app role in the `appRoles` property:\n```json\n"appRoles": [\n  {\n    "allowedMemberTypes": [\n      "User"\n    ],\n    "description": "Read access to Page1",\n    "displayName": "Page1 Readers",\n    "id": "e7d262d2-17df-4268-8c08-7d21b9eac2b1",\n    "isEnabled": true,\n    "value": "Page1_Readers",\n    "permissions": [\n      {\n        "adminConsentDescription": "Allows the application to read Page1 on your behalf",\n        "adminConsentDisplayName": "Read access to Page1",\n        "id": "9c9ef6b7-6b10-4932-9c90-2bdf47a85f1f",\n        "isEnabled": true,\n        "type": "Role",\n        "userConsentDescription": "Allow the application to read Page1 on your behalf",\n        "userConsentDisplayName": "Read access to Page1"\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n],\n```\nIn this example, the `permissions` property contains one object that defines the permission for the "Page1 Readers" app role. The `id` property is a unique identifier for the permission, and the `type` property is set to `Role`, which indicates that this is a permission role.\n\n3. Assign users or groups: Assign users or groups to each app role in Azure AD. You can assign users or groups by adding them to', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=-1, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=True, max_tokens=256, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None), prompt_token_ids: [9454, 11, 421, 697, 1614, 13798, 14576, 389, 60753, 2615, 311, 3847, 369, 3151, 6816, 323, 11574, 304, 697, 48409, 36515, 67996, 9518, 3766, 11, 498, 1265, 2908, 26312, 1817, 2150, 323, 2524, 438, 458, 906, 3476, 304, 34119, 9630, 382, 2620, 5740, 1817, 2150, 323, 2524, 438, 458, 906, 3476, 6147, 498, 311, 2524, 261