INFO 03-13 07:54:35 logger.py:39] Received request cmpl-7d993868804f4989b2effe76a0d3115c-0: prompt: "The `workspace.json` file is a configuration file that is used by Bit to manage the components in your project. It contains information about the components in your project, such as their names, versions, and dependencies.\n\nHere's how you can work with the `workspace.json` file:\n\n1. Initialize Bit in your project: Before you can start working with the `workspace.json` file, you'll need to initialize Bit in your project. This can be done by running the command `bit init` in your project's root directory. This will create a `workspace.json` file in your project.\n2. Add components to the `workspace.json` file: Once Bit is initialized in your project, you can start adding components to the `workspace.json` file. You can do this by running the command `bit add` and specifying the path to the component you want to add. Bit will automatically detect the component's dependencies and add them to the `workspace.json` file.\n3. Tag components: Once your components are added, you can tag them with a version number by running the command `bit tag`. Tagging a component allows you to keep track of changes to the component over time, and to roll back to previous versions if necessary.\n4. Export components: Once your components are tagged, you can export them by running the command `bit export`. This will make the component available for other team members or projects to import.\n5. Import components: You can import components from the `workspace.json` file into your project by running the command `bit import` and specifying the path to the component you want to import.\n6. Track the status of your components: You can use the command `bit status` to see the current status of the components in your project and check if they are up-to-date or not.\n\nIn summary, the `workspace.json` file is a configuration file that is used by Bit to manage the components in your project. It contains information about the components in your project, such as their names, versions, and dependencies. You can use various Bit commands to work with the `workspace.json` file, such as `bit init`, `bit add`, `bit tag`, `bit export`, `bit import`, and `bit status`, which will allow you to add, tag, export and import components and track the status of the components in your project.", params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=-1, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=True, max_tokens=256, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None), prompt_token_ids: [785, 1565, 42909, 4323, 63, 1034, 374, 264, 6546, 1034, 429, 374, 1483, 553, 6495, 311, 10091, 279, 6813, 304, 697, 2390, 13, 1084, 5610, 1995, 911, 279, 6813, 304, 697, 2390, 11, 1741, 438, 862, 5036, 11, 10795, 11, 323, 19543, 382, 8420, 594, 1246, 498, 646, 975, 448, 279, 1565, 42909, 4323, 63, 1034, 1447, 16, 13, 9008, 6495, 304, 697, 2390, 25, 13235, 498, 646, 1191, 3238, 448, 279, 1565, 42909, 4323, 63, 1034, 11, 498, 3278, 1184, 311, 9468, 6495, 304, 697, 2390, 13, 1096, 646, 387, 2814, 553, 4303, 279, 3210, 1565, 4489, 2930, 63, 304, 697, 2390, 594, 3704, 6220, 13, 1096, 686, 1855, 264, 1565, 42909, 4323, 63, 1034, 304, 697, 2390, 624, 17, 13, 2691, 6813, 311, 279, 1565, 42909, 4323, 63, 1034, 25, 9646, 6495, 374, 17271, 304, 697, 2390, 11, 498, 646, 1191, 7842, 6813, 311, 279, 1565, 42909, 4323, 63, 1034, 13, 1446, 646, 653, 419, 553, 4303, 279, 3210, 1565, 4489, 912, 63, 323, 37838, 279, 1815, 311, 279, 3692, 498, 1366, 311, 912, 13, 6495, 686, 9463, 11140, 279, 3692, 594, 19543, 323, 912, 1105, 311, 279, 1565, 42909, 4323, 63, 1034, 624, 18, 13, 12353, 6813, 25, 9646, 697, 6813, 525, 3694, 11, 498, 646, 4772, 1105, 448, 264, 2319, 1372