INFO 03-13 07:54:40 logger.py:39] Received request cmpl-5ca2df08a9db46fe8bde74819196cf9f-0: prompt: 'In C++, the `at` function is a member function of the `std::vector` class that is used to access the element at a specified location in the vector. It has the following signature:\n```scss\nreference at(size_type pos);\nconst_reference at(size_type pos) const;\n```\nThe `at` function takes an argument `pos` that specifies the index of the element that should be accessed. If `pos` is not a valid index, the `at` function throws an `std::out_of_range` exception.\n\nThe `at` function returns a reference to the element at the specified index, which allows the element to be modified. If the vector is a const vector, the `at` function returns a const reference to the element.\n\nFor example:\n```c\n#include <vector>\n#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    std::vector<int> v{1, 2, 3, 4, 5};\n\n    try {\n        /* access the element at index 2 */\n        int x = v.at(2);\n        std::cout << "v[2] = " << x << std::endl;\n\n        /* try to access the element at index 5 */\n        x = v.at(5);\n        std::cout << "v[5] = " << x << std::endl;\n    } catch (const std::out_of_range& e) {\n        std::cerr << "Out of range error: " << e.what() << std::endl;\n    }\n\n    return 0;\n}\n```\nIn the example above, the `at` function is used to access the element at index 2, which is 3. The `at` function then throws an `std::out_of_range` exception when it is called with an index of 5, which is not a valid index for the vector.\n\nIs there anything else you would like to know about the `at` function?', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=-1, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=True, max_tokens=256, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, 