INFO 03-13 07:54:42 logger.py:39] Received request cmpl-a182aba7f26a41e488d0c51e60111b25-0: prompt: 'You can add a video background to a `div` using HTML5\'s `<video>` element and CSS. Here\'s an example:\n```html\n<div class="video-background">\n  <video autoplay muted loop>\n    <source src="video.mp4" type="video/mp4">\n    Your browser does not support the video tag.\n  </video>\n</div>\n```\nIn this example, we\'re creating a `div` with a class of `video-background`. Inside the `div`, we\'re adding a `<video>` element with three attributes: `autoplay`, `muted`, and `loop`. These attributes will cause the video to start playing automatically, mute the audio, and loop the video when it reaches the end.\n\nWe\'re also adding a `<source>` element inside the `<video>` element with the path to the video file and its MIME type. Finally, we\'re adding a fallback message for browsers that do not support the `<video>` element.\n\nTo make the `div` fill the entire screen, you can add the following CSS:\n```css\n.video-background {\n  position: fixed;\n  top: 0;\n  left: 0;\n  width: 100%;\n  height: 100%;\n  z-index: -1;\n}\n\n.video-background video {\n  object-fit: cover;\n}\n```\nThis will position the `div` at the top-left corner of the screen, with a width and height of 100% to cover the entire screen. The `z-index` property is set to -1 to make sure the video stays in the background.\n\nThe `object-fit: cover` property in the `video` selector is used to make the video fill the entire `div` without distorting its aspect ratio.\n\nNote that different video formats may be required for different browsers. It\'s a good practice to provide multiple `<source>` elements with different video formats to ensure the video will play on different browsers.', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=-1, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output