INFO 03-13 07:54:43 logger.py:39] Received request cmpl-bbdf85d54cd94b39856c66b615786269-0: prompt: '(a) Working principle of hydraulic torque converter:\n\nA hydraulic torque converter is an automatic transmission device used to transmit power from the engine to the transmission. The hydraulic torque converter consists of an impeller, a turbine, and a stator. The impeller and turbine are connected by a fluid, which is used to transmit power. The stator redirects the fluid back to the impeller. The fluid is usually oil.\n\nWhen the engine is running, the impeller rotates and draws the fluid into the center of the torque converter. The impeller then sends the fluid to the turbine, which causes the turbine to rotate. The fluid then exits the turbine and is redirected by the stator, which causes the fluid to flow back to the impeller. The rotation of the turbine causes the output shaft to rotate and transmit power to the transmission.\n\n(b) Difference between Ackermann steering mechanism and Davis steering mechanism:\n\nAckermann steering mechanism:\n\nThe Ackermann steering mechanism is a type of steering mechanism in which the front wheels are steered in such a way that they rotate about a common center point. The inner wheel of the turn has a smaller turning radius than the outer wheel. This allows the vehicle to make a turn more easily and reduces tire wear. The Ackermann steering mechanism is commonly used in modern vehicles.\n\nDavis steering mechanism:\n\nThe Davis steering mechanism is a type of steering mechanism in which the front wheels are steered by a linkage that has two points of connection to the wheels. This type of steering mechanism is less efficient than the Ackermann steering mechanism and is not commonly used in modern vehicles. The Davis steering mechanism causes the wheels to turn at different angles, which can cause tire wear and can make it difficult to control the vehicle.', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty