INFO 03-13 07:54:45 logger.py:39] Received request cmpl-4ca9ef1776d04fc6a629da7e694d96df-0: prompt: 'Yes, you can reuse the `item_api` instance instead of creating a new `variation_api` instance for each variation. Here\'s the updated script:\n```ruby\nrequire \'square_connect\'\nrequire \'csv\'\n\n# Replace with your Square access token\nSquareConnect.configure do |config|\n  config.access_token = \'YOUR_ACCESS_TOKEN\'\nend\n\n# Replace with your Square location ID\nlocation_id = \'YOUR_LOCATION_ID\'\n\n# Define the custom attribute name\ncustom_attribute_name = \'avalara_tax_id\'\n\n# Load the spreadsheet data\nbegin\n  spreadsheet_data = CSV.read(\'spreadsheet.csv\', headers: true)\nrescue StandardError => e\n  puts "Error reading CSV file: #{e.message}"\n  exit\nend\n\n# Create an instance of the Square Catalog API\nitem_api = SquareConnect::CatalogApi.new\n\n# Loop through each row in the spreadsheet\nspreadsheet_data.each do |row|\n  # Get the Square item ID and custom attribute value from the spreadsheet\n  item_id = row[\'square_item_id\']\n  custom_attribute_value = row[\'avalara_tax_id\']\n\n  # Retrieve the item and its variations from Square\n  begin\n    item_response = item_api.retrieve_catalog_object(item_id)\n  rescue StandardError => e\n    puts "Error retrieving item #{item_id}: #{e.message}"\n    next\n  end\n\n  # Check if the object is an item or an item variation\n  if item_response.object_type == \'ITEM\'\n    # Update the item\'s custom attribute value\n    item_response.custom_attribute_values ||= []\n    item_response.custom_attribute_values << SquareConnect::CatalogCustomAttributeValue.new(\n      custom_attribute_definition_id: custom_attribute_name,\n      string_value: custom_attribute_value\n    )\n    begin\n      item_api.upsert_catalog_object(item_response, idempotency_key: SecureRandom.uuid)\n    rescue StandardError => e\n      puts "Error updating item #{item_id}: #{e.message}"\n      next\n    end\n  elsif item_response.object_type == \'ITEM_VARIATION\'\n    # Update the variation\'s custom attribute value\n    variation_id = item_response.id\n    item_id = item_response.item_variation_data.item_id\n    item_response = item_api.retrieve_catalog_object(item_id)\n    variation_response = item_response.item_variation_data.find { |variation| variation.id == variation_id }\n\n    variation_response.custom_attribute_values ||= []\n    variation_response.custom_attribute_values << SquareConnect::CatalogCustomAttributeValue.new(\n      custom_attribute_definition_id: custom_attribute_name,\n      string_value: custom_attribute_value\n    )\n    begin\n      item_api.upsert_catalog_object(item_response, idempotency_key: SecureRandom.uuid)\n    rescue StandardError => e\n      puts "Error updating variation #{variation_id}: #{e.message}"\n      next\n    end\n  end\nend\n```\nIn this version of the script, we create a single instance of the `CatalogApi` class and reuse it for all API calls instead of creating a new instance of `CatalogApi` for each variation. When updating a variation, we retrieve the corresponding item object from Square and then find the specific variation within that object to update its custom attribute value. This should improve performance and simplify the code.', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=-1, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=True, max_tokens=256, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None), prompt_token_ids: [9454, 11, 498, 646, 25978, 279, 1565, 1203, 11697, 63, 2867, 4518, 315, 6825, 264, 501, 1565, 75785, 11697, 63, 2867, 369, 1817, 22990, 13, 5692, 594, 279, 6049, 5316, 510, 73594, 46275, 198, 4310, 364, 37476, 15720, 1248, 4310, 364, 18104, 3876, 2, 29558, 448, 697, 15619, 26