INFO 03-13 07:54:54 logger.py:39] Received request cmpl-69ef1310511d4b1bb63e813d01032a3f-0: prompt: "To continuously send the latitude and longitude values over UDP in TypeScript, you can use a `setInterval` function to send the data at a specified interval. Here's an example code snippet:\n```typescript\nimport * as dgram from 'dgram';\n\nconst client = dgram.createSocket('udp4');\n\nconst PORT = 1234;\nconst HOST = '127.0.0.1';\n\n// Define a function to send the data\nfunction sendData() {\n  const latitude = 37.7749;\n  const longitude = -122.4194;\n\n  // Encode latitude and longitude as a hex string\n  const latHex = latitude.toString(16).padStart(8, '0');\n  const longHex = longitude.toString(16).padStart(8, '0');\n  const hexString = latHex + longHex;\n\n  // Convert hex string to buffer\n  const data = Buffer.from(hexString, 'hex');\n\n  // Send the data over UDP\n  client.send(data, 0, data.length, PORT, HOST, (err) => {\n    if (err) {\n      console.error(err);\n    } else {\n      console.log(`Sent data to ${HOST}:${PORT}`);\n    }\n  });\n}\n\n// Send the data every 1 second\nsetInterval(sendData, 1000);\n```\nIn this example, we define a function `sendData` that encodes the latitude and longitude values as a hex string, converts the hex string to a buffer, and sends the resulting data packet over UDP using `client.send` method. We then use `setInterval` function to call the `sendData` function every 1 second.\n\nNote that in this example, we are sending the data to a local host on port 1234. You will need to replace the `HOST` and `PORT` values with the appropriate values for your use case. Also, ensure that the server is running and listening on the specified port to receive the data.", params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=-1, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=True, max_tokens=256, mi