INFO 03-13 07:55:01 logger.py:39] Received request cmpl-c178e0d895844802b0e68f5196977a35-0: prompt: 'Sure, to answer the question "What are the factors that impact the test score?", you can perform a multiple linear regression analysis. Here\'s an outline of the steps you can take starting from point 8:\n\n8. Use the `predict()` function to predict the test scores for new data based on the model you have created:\n```\nnewdata <- data.frame(computer = mean(Caschool$computer), expnstu = mean(Caschool$expnstu), str = mean(Caschool$str), avginc = mean(Caschool$avginc), elpct = mean(Caschool$elpct))\npredictions <- predict(model, newdata)\n```\nThis will give you an estimate of the test score based on the mean values of the independent variables.\n\n9. Use the `summary()` and `confint()` function to check the model\'s performance and check how the model is generalizing to new data\n```\nsummary(model)\nconfint(model)\n```\n10. Use the `plot()` function to visualize the residuals\n```\nplot(model)\n```\nThis will give you a visual representation of the residuals and help you to identify if there are any patterns or outliers that need to be addressed.\n\n11. Use the `rsquared()` function to check the goodness of fit of the model\n```\nrsq <- rsquared(model)\n```\nBy following these steps, you should be able to identify the factors that impact the test score and understand how they relate to one another.\nPlease let me know if there\'s anything else I can do to help or if you have any other question.', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=-1, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=True, max_tokens=256, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None), prompt_token_ids: [39814, 11, 311, 4226, 279, 34