INFO 03-13 07:55:09 logger.py:39] Received request cmpl-816a41465e6b41368a5f75f75039fc49-0: prompt: 'To create a Flask app, you can follow these steps:\n\n1. Make sure that you have activated your virtual environment in the terminal. If the virtual environment is not active, you can use the `source` or `.` (dot) command to activate it. For example:\n```\n. myenv/Scripts/activate\n```\n2. Use the `flask new` subcommand to create a new Flask app. For example, to create a new Flask app named "myapp", you can use the following `flask` command:\n```\nflask new myapp\n```\n3. Use the `cd` command to navigate to the app\'s directory. For example:\n```\ncd myapp\n```\n4. Use the `flask run` subcommand to start the Flask development server. For example:\n```\nflask run\n```\n5. Open a web browser and navigate to `http://localhost:5000` to access the Flask app.\n\nWhile you are working on your Flask app, you will need to keep the virtual environment active in the terminal so that the Flask app can use the installed packages. You can deactivate the virtual environment at any time by using the `deactivate` command in the terminal. When you are ready to resume work on your Flask app, you can reactivate the virtual environment using the `source` or `.` (dot) command, as described in step 1.\n\nIn summary, these are the steps to create a Flask app:\n\n1. Activate the virtual environment using the `source` or `.` (dot) command.', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=-1, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=True, max_tokens=256, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None), prompt_token_ids: [1249, 1855, 264, 28173, 906, 11, 498, 646, 1795, 1493, 7354, 1447, 16, 13, 7405, 2704, 429, 498, 614, 21985, 697