INFO 03-13 07:55:10 logger.py:39] Received request cmpl-2a0fe32dbe504371bea6a87678d6c971-0: prompt: 'Building a scanner with ESP32 and controlling it with Bluetooth is a great idea. Here are the steps to build the scanner and control it with Bluetooth:\n\n1. Hardware setup: Connect the LEDs to the ESP32 board. You can use any of the available digital pins on the board, but make sure to note down the pins you\'re using as you\'ll need to reference them in the code. You will also need to connect a resistor to each LED to limit the current and prevent damage to the ESP32.\n2. Install the necessary libraries: You will need to install the "FastLED" library to control the LEDs and the "BluetoothSerial" library to enable Bluetooth communication. To install the libraries, go to Sketch > Include Library > Manage Libraries, and search for the libraries. Click on "Install" to install them.\n3. Write the code: Here\'s a sample code that scans the LEDs in a pattern and allows you to adjust the speed of the scanner using Bluetooth. The code uses the "FastLED" library to control the LEDs and the "BluetoothSerial" library to enable Bluetooth communication.\n```c++\n#include <FastLED.h>\n#include <BluetoothSerial.h>\n\n// Set the number of LEDs and the pin they\'re connected to\n#define NUM_LEDS 10\n#define LED_PIN 5\n\n// Define the LED strip\nCRGB leds[NUM_LEDS];\n\n// Define the Bluetooth object\nBluetoothSerial SerialBT;\n\n// Set the initial scanner speed and the maximum and minimum speeds\nint speed = 50;\nint max_speed = 200;\nint min_speed = 10;\n\nvoid setup() {\n  // Initialize the LED strip\n  FastLED.addLeds<WS2812, LED_PIN, GRB>(leds, NUM_LEDS);\n\n  // Start the Bluetooth communication\n  SerialBT.begin("ESP32 Scanner");\n}\n\nvoid loop() {\n  // Adjust the speed of the scanner based on the Bluetooth input\n  if (SerialBT.available()) {\n    char input = SerialBT.read();\n    if (input == \'+\') {\n      speed += 10;\n      if (speed > max_speed) speed = max_speed;\n    } else if (input == \'-\') {\n      speed -= 10;\n      if (speed < min_speed) speed = min_speed;\n    }\n  }\n\n  // Cycle through the LEDs in a pattern\n  for (int i = 0; i < NUM_LEDS; i++) {\n    leds[i] = CRGB::Red;\n    FastLED.show();\n    delay(speed);\n\n    leds[i] = CRGB::Black;\n    FastLED.show();\n  }\n\n  for (int i = NUM_LEDS - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n    leds[i] = CRGB::Red;\n    FastLED.show();\n    delay(speed);\n\n    leds[i] = CRGB::Black;\n    FastLED.show();\n  }\n}\n```\n1. Upload the code to the ESP32 board: Once you\'ve written the code, compile and upload it to the ESP32 board using the Arduino IDE.\n2. Test the scanner: Power on the ESP32 board and use your smartphone or computer to connect to the Bluetooth device named "ESP32 Scanner". Once connected, you can send the \'+\' and \'-\' characters to adjust the speed of the scanner.\n\nWith these steps, you can build a scanner with ESP32 and control it with Bluetooth. You can also modify the code to add more advanced features such as motion detection, sound responsiveness, or color control.', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=-1, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=True, max_tokens=256, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None), prompt_token_ids: [30133, 264, 20775, 448, 18117, 18, 17, 323, 25902, 432, 448, 23843, 374, 264, 2244, 4522, 13, 5692, 525, 279, 7354, 311, 1936, 279, 20775, 323, 2524, 432, 448, 23843, 1447, 16, 13, 36765, 6505, 25, 13015, 279, 55572, 311, 279, 18117, 18, 17, 4479, 13, 1446, 646, 990, 894, 315, 279, 2500, 7377, 26944, 389, 279, 4479, 11, 714, 1281, 2704, 311, 5185, 1495, 279, 26944, 498, 2299, 1667, 438, 498, 3278, 1184, 311, 5785, 1105, 304, 279, 2038, 13, 1446, 686, 1083, 1184, 31