INFO 03-13 07:55:10 logger.py:39] Received request cmpl-143b9a727a7142a9becc20e4468cc167-0: prompt: 'The next concept is:\n\n5. Observables\n--------------\n\nObservables are a powerful way to handle asynchronous data streams in Angular. They are used extensively in Angular for things like fetching data from a backend API or reacting to user events.\n\nAn observable is a stream of data that can be observed over time. You can subscribe to an observable to receive notifications whenever new data is available.\n\nHere\'s an example of how to use an observable in Angular:\n```typescript\nimport { Component } from \'@angular/core\';\nimport { Observable } from \'rxjs\';\nimport { UserService } from \'./user.service\';\n\n@Component({\n  selector: \'app-user-list\',\n  template: `\n    <ul>\n      <li *ngFor="let user of users$ | async">{{ user }}</li>\n    </ul>\n  `\n})\nexport class UserListComponent {\n  users$: Observable<string[]>;\n\n  constructor(private userService: UserService) {\n    this.users$ = this.userService.getUsers();\n  }\n}\n```\nIn this example, we have a `UserListComponent` that displays a list of users. The `UserListComponent` has a dependency on the `UserService`, which provides an observable that emits an array of users.\n\nTo use the `Observable` provided by the `UserService`, we assign it to a property in the `UserListComponent`. We also use the `async` pipe in the template to subscribe to the observable and display the emitted data in the view.\n\nHere are some questions to test your understanding of observables:\n\n1. What are observables?\n2. How are observables used in Angular?\n3. What is the difference between an observable and a promise?\n4. What is the `async` pipe used for?\n\nLet me know if you have any questions, or if you\'re ready to move on to the next concept!', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=-1, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop