INFO 03-13 07:55:14 logger.py:39] Received request cmpl-3e037edbde2a44f8b2b92ca8049b3568-0: prompt: 'Here is the remaining part of the code for the `convertWhereClause` method:\n```\n// Extract the left hand side, operator, and right hand side of the WHERE clause\nMatcher whereMatcher = WHERE_PATTERN.matcher(whereClause);\nif (whereMatcher.find()) {\n  String lhs = whereMatcher.group(1);\n  String op = whereMatcher.group(2);\n  String rhs = whereMatcher.group(3);\n\n  // Convert the SQL operator to a MongoDB operator\n  op = OPERATOR_MAP.get(op);\n\n  // Return the MongoDB filter\n  return String.format("%s: {%s: %s}", lhs, op, rhs);\n}\n\nreturn "";\n```\nThis method uses a regular expression pattern to extract the left hand side, operator, and right hand side of the WHERE clause. It then converts the SQL operator to a MongoDB operator using the `OPERATOR_MAP`, and returns a MongoDB filter with the converted operator.\n\nHere is the code for the `convertJoinClause` method, which converts a SQL JOIN clause to a MongoDB lookup stage:\n```\n// Extract the left and right collection and field names from the JOIN clause\nMatcher joinMatcher = JOIN_PATTERN.matcher(joinClause);\nif (joinMatcher.find()) {\n  String leftCollection = joinMatcher.group(1);\n  String leftField = joinMatcher.group(2);\n  String rightField = joinMatcher.group(3);\n\n  // Return the MongoDB lookup stage\n  return String.format(", {$lookup: {from: \'%s\', localField: \'%s\', foreignField: \'%s\', as: \'%s\'}}",\n                       leftCollection, leftField, rightField, leftCollection);\n}\n\nreturn "";\n```\nThis method uses a regular expression pattern to extract the left and right collection and field names from the JOIN clause. It then returns a MongoDB lookup stage with these values.\n\nHere is the code for the `convertHavingClause` method, which converts a SQL HAVING clause to a MongoDB filter:\n```\n// Extract the left hand side, operator, and right hand side of the HAVING clause\nMatcher havingMatcher = WHERE_PATTERN.matcher(havingClause);\nif (havingMatcher.find()) {\n  String lhs = havingMatcher.group(1);\n  String op = havingMatcher.group(2);\n  String rhs = havingMatcher.group(3);\n\n  // Convert the SQL operator to a MongoDB operator\n  op = OPERATOR_MAP.get(op);\n\n  // Return the MongoDB filter\n  return String.format("%s: {%s: %s}", lhs, op, rhs);\n}\n\nreturn "";\n```\nThis method is similar to the `convertWhereClause` method, but it uses a regular expression pattern for the HAVING clause instead of the WHERE clause. It extracts the left hand side, operator, and right hand side of the HAVING clause, converts the SQL operator to a MongoDB operator, and returns a MongoDB filter with the converted operator.', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=-1, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=True, max_tokens=256, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None), prompt_token_ids: [8420, 374, 279, 9664, 949, 315, 279, 2038, 369, 279, 1565, 14166, 9064, 28482, 63, 1714, 510, 13874, 3989, 322, 22826, 279, 2115, 1424, 3108, 11, 5675, 11, 323, 1290, 1424, 3108, 315, 279, 5288, 21647, 198, 37554, 1380, 37554, 284, 5288, 44227, 49227, 58433, 28482, 317, 333, 320, 2870, 37554, 2658, 2140, 341, 220, 923, 22505, 284, 1380, 37554, 6175, 7, 16, 317, 220, 923, 1179, 284, 1380, 37554, 6175, 7, 17, 317, 220, 923, 12916, 284, 1380, 37554, 6175, 7, 18, 626, 220, 442, 7169, 279, 7870, 5675, 311, 264, 45328, 5675, 198, 220, 1179, 284, 41376, 11857, 16306, 670, 17096, 626, 220, 442, 3411, 279, 45328, 4051, 198, 220, 470, 923, 8021, 4430, 82, 25, 52261, 82, 25, 1018, 82, 9545, 22505, 11, 1179, 11, 12916, 317, 630, 689, 5438, 13874, 3989, 1986, 1714, 5711, 264, 5792, 7493, 5383, 311, 8649, 279,