INFO 03-13 07:55:22 logger.py:39] Received request cmpl-5e2367156eae4e3a8ba695b7f81841d2-0: prompt: "Yes, there are several ways to visualize the recursion process in the `createTree` function. One way to do this is to use a tree diagram to represent the tree data structure as it is being built.\n\nFor example, suppose we have the following array of `Question` objects:\n```javascript\nconst questions = [\n  { id: 1, key: 'name', parent: null, order: 1 },\n  { id: 2, key: 'age', parent: 1, order: 2 },\n  { id: 3, key: 'email', parent: 1, order: 3 },\n  { id: 4, key: 'address', parent: null, order: 4 },\n  { id: 5, key: 'street', parent: 4, order: 1 },\n  { id: 6, key: 'city', parent: 4, order: 2 },\n  { id: 7, key: 'state', parent: 6, order: 1 },\n  { id: 8, key: 'zip', parent: 6, order: 2 },\n];\n```\nWe can visualize the recursion process by drawing a tree diagram that represents the tree structure as it is being built. We can start by drawing the root node(s) of the tree, which are the `Question` objects that have a `parent` property of `null`.\n```css\n              name          address\n```\nWe can then recursively add the children of each node to the diagram, by calling the `createTree` function on each child node.\n\nFirst, we call the `createTree` function on the `name` node, which has two children: `age` and `email`.\n```css\n              name          address\n               |\n           +---+---+\n           |       |\n          age    email\n```\nNext, we call the `createTree` function on the `address` node, which has two children: `street` and `city`.\n```lua\n              name          address\n               |              |\n           +---+---+      +---+---+\n           |       |      |       |\n          age    email  street  city\n                               |\n                              +---+\n                              |   |\n                             state zip\n```\nThis process continues until all nodes have been added to the tree.\n\nAs for resources to help you understand recursion better, there are many tutorials and articles available online that explain the concept of recursion and how it works. Here are a few resources that you might find helpful:\n\n* [Recursion explained with the flood-fill algorithm](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4uG1NMKNWCU) - A video that explains the concept of recursion using the flood-fill algorithm.\n* [Recursion in JavaScript](https://www.freecodecamp.org/news/recursion-in-javascript-1608032c7a1f/) - An article that explains how recursion works in JavaScript and provides examples of recursive functions.\n* [Recursion explained visually](https://www.cs.usfca.edu/~galles/visualization/Recursion.html) - An interactive visualization that shows how recursion works using a tree diagram.\n* [Visualizing recursion with Python tutor](http://pythontutor.com/visualize.html#mode=edit) - A tool that allows you to visualize how recursive functions work by stepping through the code and displaying the call stack.", params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=-1, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=True, max_tokens=256, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None), prompt_token_ids: [9454, 11, 1052, 525, 3807, 5510, 311, 50087, 279, 50262, 1882, 304, 279, 1565, 3182, 6533, 63, 729, 13, 3776, 1616, 311, 653, 419, 374, 311, 990, 264, 4916, 13549, 311, 4009, 279, 4916, 821, 5944, 438, 432, 374, 1660, 5798, 382, 2461, 3110, 11, 22477, 582, 614, 279, 2701, 1334, 315, 1565, 14582, 63, 6171, 510, 73594, 14073, 198, 1024, 4755, 284, 2278, 220, 314, 877, 25, 220, 16, 11, 1376, 25, 364, 606, 516, 2681, 25, 845, 11, 1973, 25, 220, 16, 1153, 220, 314, 877, 25, 220, 17, 11, 1376, 25, 364, 424, 516, 2681, 25, 2